eptually open to  nuclear as a bridge but given how long  it takes to actually start producing new  nuclear power I don't even know that the  timeline makes sense for us  geopolitically this is all really  important stuff if we reduce our demand  for fossil fuels this weakens the  leverage that country's hostile to us  have a Venezuela Russia I'm not saying  we should never try to have good  relationships with these countries I'm  merely saying that deleveraging them  when it comes to manipulation and  control over the oil markets would be a  very good thing  geopolitically and a very good thing in  terms of national security yes there may  be short-term pains in terms of gas  taxes or other costs that would be part  of a long term solution but I think that  if we think through it carefully the  government can ensure the  that those who would be most hurt by a  slight uptick in the cost of gas or  other fuel energy sources we can  mitigate those impacts and I think that  we should if I'm missing something here  send me your thoughts I want to hear  from you new homes have had the option  of building with solar power for a long  time in the United States and the cost  of doing so has come down over time but  California is going to make solar power  a standard requirement on all new homes  built in their state and the day after  tomorrow the California Energy  Commission will be voting on new energy  standards for new homes and they're  expected to mandate solar panels on  homes built starting in 2020 so this all  sounds great except that there are two  camps that oppose this both on the left  and on the right so you've got the right  wing anti-regulation types who say they  just don't want more regulation and the  government telling people how they have  to build homes of course there's always  been regulation or at least for a long  time on new homes whether it's  insulation standards or heating  efficiency standards whatever this is  just the evolution of those standards I  don't think that's a very good argument  but there are also people sort of on the  Left who wanted a net zero requirement  they wanted Net Zero to be the standard  meaning that the house has to produce  more solar power than it uses not hugely  realistic because right now our ability  to store solar energy is pretty limited  so at night when the Sun is down homes  even with normal solar installations  need to pull electricity from the grid  so the decision was made that's a little  too much right now let's mandate some  solar of course there will be some  exceptions and that's almost always the  case here and the important thing to  remember is yeah even if really rich  people building mansions are going to  potentially be able to get exceptions or  some large commercial office buildings  or I'm sorry not commercial leased  buildings least residential buildings  we'll be able to get exemptions overall  this is a move in the right direction  and like with any market that has new  regulation people will be able to get  around it some people will but the big  picture is a really really good one  and I think that there's another  interesting element here that people  often like to point out which is  okay fine you put the solar panels on  and the energy is solar but you need  fossil fuels to make the solar panels  and you've got all these solar  installers driving around burning  gasoline even if they have electric cars  that they're driving around in the  electricity comes from fossil fuels  we've dealt with that argument many  times before that needs to be figured  out and it eventually will be but in the  short term let's at least make the move  to solar power and then we'll continue  to work on reducing the carbon footprint  and the fossil fuel footprint of the  creation and installation of the solar  panels like that that's one of those  examples where people say well there's  still fossil fuels in the system for  doing solar panel installation yes there 